    "preparation": "The exact full planned output and decoded applied query are persisted before the plan is returned. Original owner/base and latest draft remain separate; later draft uploads cannot erase the intent.",
    "reconciliation": "A restored session is paused. Explicit resume accepts only the original owner or exact prepared output, with fresh source/descriptor/context/policy checks and native counters allowed to restart. No source edit is replayed.",
    "partialSave": "An already applied dirty XML model can resume against the original disk even if the original plan asked the adapter to save. A clean model must match the exact on-disk output including its BOM.",
    "cleanup": "Abort clears the intent with checkpoint CAS; close releases only memory and leaves recovery data. Default removal rejects pending_apply; API 1.16 discardPrepared:true explicitly discards the exact checkpoint revision.",
    "readWithoutMetadata": "recoveryPreparedRead is bounded to 128 KiB UTF-8 chunks and remains available after initialize with an invalid manifest.",
    "ownerExclusivity": "Opening a new source handle or restoring another checkpoint for the same known owner cannot bypass its unreconciled intent.",
    "storage": "Ordinary checkpoints stay v1. Pending v2 envelopes use canonical base64 or validated local references to ordinary parts in the same record; no paths, cross-record or recursive references are allowed. Reads do not rewrite old files.",
    "limits": "Existing 32 MiB retained-text, 8 MiB part and 64 MiB encoded scan/file limits remain unchanged. Shared copies do not inflate canonical files beyond the scan bound.",
    "sourceFilesWrittenByBackend": false,
    "newDependencies": false,
    "compatibility": "Negotiated 1.15 retains documented memory-only prepare/abort and preparedStateDurable:false. Clients 1.14-1.15 are shown pending records as unsupported/damaged and preserve their files; ordinary v1 recovery remains available.",
    "headlessBsl": "Core crash reconciliation also checks the normalized LF baseline against an exact CRLF draft and a caller-supplied new lock acknowledgement. This does not prove native BSL input prevention."
  },
